<title>USA: U.S. Dec wholesale inventories fell 0.6 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Dec wholesale inventories fell 0.6 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-02-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Commerce Department report of wholesale inventories and sales, seasonally adjusted.</p>
<p> (Percent Changes)     Dec    Nov--Rev from  Dec96/95</p>
<p> Inventories Total    -0.6    0.1     0.1     0.5</p>
<p> Sales Total	     0.2    0.6     0.9     4.9</p>
<p> (Billions of dollars)   Dec     Nov--Rev from   Dec95</p>
<p> Inventories Total  256.34  257.93  258.02   254.96</p>
<p> -Durable Goods     162.44  163.48  163.58   161.93</p>
<p> --Cars/Parts	  26.46   27.58   27.59    27.31</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods  93.90   94.45   94.43    93.03</p>
<p> --Petroleum Prods.   5.78    5.55    5.49     4.93</p>
<p> (Billions of dollars)   Dec     Nov--Rev from   Dec95</p>
<p>  Sales Total	 204.34  203.93  204.58   194.84</p>
<p> -Durable Goods     104.42  104.55  105.35   101.82</p>
<p> --Cars/Parts	  17.44   17.04   16.86    17.20</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods  99.92   99.38   99.23    93.02</p>
<p> --Petroleum Prods.  17.14   16.92   16.95    13.60</p>
<p>For all of 1996, total U.S. wholesale sales rose 6.7 percent from 1995 to $2.399 trillion, the department said.</p>
<p>The December stock-to-sales ratio for merchant wholesalers, seasonally adjusted, dropped to 1.25 months' worth from 1.26 months' worth in November. The department said it was the lowest ratio for any month since May 1985, when it also was 1.25 months' worth.</p>
<p>The ratio is a measure of how many months it would take to sell existing inventories at the current sales pace.</p>
